// Module 04 — Operations and Projects (specs/04-operations-projects.md §3).
//
// §2 is firm about vocabulary, and it is not a style preference: "A **Ticket** is the unit of
// operational work generated from a PO… This is the company's own term — use it in the UI, the
// code, and the numbering. **Do not rename it to 'job order'.**"
//
// §1 draws two things that shape these models more than anything else:
//
//  1. **The delivery lane is genuinely separate.** It has its own mobilization, its own
//     demobilization and its own retry loops. "It is not a step inside a project — it is a ticket
//     type. Model it as such." So a delivery ticket has no `projectId`, and that is a rule rather
//     than an omission.
//  2. **Four gates**, two before mobilization (cash advance, material request) and two after
//     execution (QA, warranty). Session 1 carries the columns they move; the gates themselves are
//     later sessions, and the columns are here so those sessions need no migration.

/// The unit of operational work (§2, §3).
///
/// Generated from a sales order — but never *silently*. §4: "Operations **confirms or edits** the
/// proposed set before generation. Do not auto-generate silently — one PO can legitimately be one
/// ticket or eight, and only a human knows which." So nothing in this build creates a Ticket from an
/// event; `sales_order.created` produces a proposal for somebody to review.
model Ticket {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESTKT-{YY}{####}, seeded since module 00.
  number String @unique

  /// Where it came from. All nullable: §4 allows a standalone ticket with no PO at all — "warranty
  /// callback, emergency, goodwill visit" — and those are real work that still needs scheduling.
  salesOrderId String?
  customerPOId String?
  accountId    String
  siteId       String?

  /// new_project | installation | after_sales | delivery (§3). The type is the route: §1's four
  /// lanes hang off it, and a delivery ticket runs an entirely different one.
  type     String
  /// For after_sales: warranty | corrective | preventive | calibration | troubleshooting | training.
  subType  String?
  /// low | normal | high | emergency
  priority String  @default("normal")

  title               String
  scopeOfWork         String
  specialInstructions String?

  /// generated | cash_advance_pending | material_pending | ready_to_mobilize | mobilized |
  /// in_progress | qa | tc | for_closeout | completed | cancelled | on_hold (§3).
  ///
  /// The full vocabulary from the start, though session 1 only reaches `generated`. The same choice
  /// `CustomerPO.status` made: a column with its whole vocabulary lets the sessions that add the
  /// gates add behaviour without a migration.
  status String @default("generated")

  /// Null for delivery tickets, always (§2). Several tickets can roll up to one project.
  projectId String?

  raisedById String
  raisedAt   DateTime @default(now())

  assignedLeadId  String?
  /// Plain ids, not a join: read whole, never queried across. Same choice as `productLines`.
  assignedUserIds String[] @default([])

  requiredByDate DateTime?
  holdReason     String?

  /// §1's first gate. A boolean rather than a status because the *question* is asked of every
  /// ticket type — "CASH ADVANCE REQUIRED? (Y/N)" — and the answer being "no" is a decision worth
  /// recording, not an absence.
  cashAdvanceRequired   Boolean @default(false)
  /// §1's second gate: undecided | required | not_applicable | requested | issued | partial.
  ///
  /// **Defaults to `undecided`, and that default was wrong until 2026-08-17.** It was
  /// `not_applicable`, which made every freshly generated ticket read as though somebody had
  /// considered the materials question and answered no. §7 forbids exactly that confusion: "`N/A` is
  /// a legitimate, recorded answer — **not a skipped step**. The record shows someone decided."
  ///
  /// A default that asserts a decision nobody made is worse than a null: the gate opened, the ticket
  /// looked ready, and the crew would have found out at the store. `undecided` is not in §3's list
  /// because §3 assumed the question always gets asked; §7 is the section that says it must be
  /// recorded when it is.
  materialRequestStatus String  @default("undecided")

  /// §4: a standalone ticket is "`billable = false` by default and a required justification".
  /// Nobody is invoiced for a goodwill visit, and the reason it happened is the whole record of it.
  billable      Boolean @default(true)
  justification String?

  customFields Json @default("{}")

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  account               CustomerAccount        @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])
  site                  Site?                  @relation(fields: [siteId], references: [id])
  salesOrder            SalesOrder?            @relation(fields: [salesOrderId], references: [id])
  customerPO            CustomerPO?            @relation(fields: [customerPOId], references: [id])
  project               Project?               @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])
  lines                 TicketSalesOrderLine[]
  cashAdvances          CashAdvance[]
  inspections           SiteInspection[]
  methodologies         Methodology[]
  materialRequests      MaterialRequest[]
  mobilizations         Mobilization[]
  dailyProgress         DailyProgress[]
  qaApprovals           QAApproval[]
  testingCommissionings TestingCommissioning[]
  serviceReports        ServiceReport[]
  deliveryFlow          DeliveryTicketFlow?

  @@index([accountId, status])
  @@index([salesOrderId])
  @@index([status, requiredByDate])
  @@index([assignedLeadId, status])
}

/// Which sales order lines a ticket covers (§4).
///
/// **Not in §3's model sketch, and required by §4**: "Each ticket links back to the specific sales
/// order lines it covers, so fulfilment counters and billing milestones stay accurate." Without it,
/// a sales order that generated three tickets cannot say which one owes which line — and module 05
/// cannot bill a milestone, because a milestone is defined by the lines it covers.
///
/// A join table rather than an id array on `Ticket`, because this is the one relation in the module
/// that other modules will query *across* — "which ticket covers this line" is module 05's question,
/// and an array cannot answer it with an index.
model TicketSalesOrderLine {
  id               String @id @default(cuid())
  ticketId         String
  /// Plain id: `SalesOrderLine` is module 03's, and the modules stay uncoupled at the schema level
  /// the same way `PrincipalProspect.supplierId` did until module 03 landed. It becomes a foreign
  /// key when something needs the database to enforce it.
  salesOrderLineId String

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  ticket Ticket @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// One ticket covers a line once. Two tickets may legitimately cover the same line — a supply and
  /// a separate commissioning visit — so the uniqueness is per pair, not per line.
  @@unique([ticketId, salesOrderLineId])
  @@index([salesOrderLineId])
}

/// The container for field execution (§2, §3).
///
/// §2: "A **Project** exists only for `new_project`, `installation`, and `after_sales` tickets that
/// involve field execution… A single PO can generate several tickets, and several tickets can roll
/// up to one project." So a project is created *with* the first execution ticket of an order rather
/// than per ticket — three visits to the same site for the same order are one project with one
/// schedule, one team and one close-out pack.
///
/// A delivery ticket never has one.
model Project {
  id   String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESPRJ-{YY}{####}.
  code String @unique

  salesOrderId String?
  accountId    String
  siteId       String?

  name        String
  description String?
  scopeOfWork String

  /// planning | site_inspection | methodology | mobilising | in_progress | qa | tc | for_closeout |
  /// closed | on_hold | cancelled (§3). Session 1 reaches `planning`; §6's site inspection and
  /// methodology gates move it from there.
  status String @default("planning")

  plannedStart DateTime?
  plannedEnd   DateTime?
  actualStart  DateTime?
  actualEnd    DateTime?

  projectManagerId String?
  teamMemberIds    String[] @default([])

  /// Permission-gated on the way out, exactly as the quotation's and the sales order's are
  /// (Spec.md §4.3). §19: "Technicians… see scope, site data, and their own cash advances — **never
  /// contract value or margin**."
  contractValue Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  budgetCost    Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  actualCost    Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 2)

  progressPct Int     @default(0)
  riskLevel   String  @default("low")
  holdReason  String?

  customFields Json @default("{}")

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  account               CustomerAccount        @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])
  site                  Site?                  @relation(fields: [siteId], references: [id])
  tickets               Ticket[]
  cashAdvances          CashAdvance[]
  inspections           SiteInspection[]
  methodologies         Methodology[]
  materialRequests      MaterialRequest[]
  mobilizations         Mobilization[]
  dailyProgress         DailyProgress[]
  qaApprovals           QAApproval[]
  testingCommissionings TestingCommissioning[]
  serviceReports        ServiceReport[]
  closeOut              ProjectCloseOut?
  warrantyClaims        WarrantyClaim[]

  @@index([accountId, status])
  @@index([status, plannedStart])
}

/// §5's Gate 1 — the cash advance that stands between a ticket and a crew leaving the yard.
///
/// §5 opens by naming why it is modelled at all: "This is a real constraint in the flowchart and is
/// **currently invisible to everyone until a technician can't board a bus**. Make it a blocking,
/// tracked step."
///
/// So the money, the approval, the release and the liquidation are one record with one status, and
/// the ticket's `cashAdvanceRequired` flag is what turns it into a gate.
model CashAdvance {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESCA-{YY}{####}, seeded since module 00.
  number String @unique

  /// One of the two, usually the ticket. §5 allows a project-level advance for work that spans
  /// several tickets — "covering the whole crew on one advance".
  ticketId  String?
  projectId String?

  requestedById String
  /// The crew this covers. §5: "The request comes from the assigned team leader or the Operations
  /// Manager, **covering the whole crew on one advance**" — so five technicians on one job produce
  /// one advance, not five, and the liquidation that follows is one reconciliation.
  requestedFor  String[] @default([])

  purpose   String
  /// [{ category, description, amount }] — §5's eight categories, in a shape the screen edits as a
  /// table. Json rather than a line model because nothing queries across a breakdown line: it is
  /// read whole with its advance, and the liquidation is what gets reconciled against it.
  breakdown Json   @default("[]")

  amountRequested Decimal  @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  amountApproved  Decimal? @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  currency        String   @default("PHP")

  /// When the crew needs the money in hand — not when they leave. §5's whole point is that the two
  /// are different and nobody currently sees the gap.
  neededBy DateTime

  /// draft | pending_approval | approved | rejected | released | liquidated |
  /// partially_liquidated | overdue_liquidation | extended (§5).
  status String @default("draft")

  approvedById    String?
  approvedAt      DateTime?
  rejectionReason String?

  /// [{ requestedAt, requestedById, reason, newDueAt, approvedById, approvedAt }] (§5).
  ///
  /// An **append-only log**, never a silent edit of `liquidationDueAt`. §5 is explicit about why:
  /// "an indefinitely extendable deadline becomes no deadline unless the extension itself is
  /// visible and counted." The register reads this to tell *formally extended, and why* apart from
  /// *simply late*.
  extensions Json @default("[]")

  releasedById  String?
  releasedAt    DateTime?
  /// cash | bank_transfer | gcash | petty_cash (§5).
  releaseMethod String?

  /// §5: "Liquidation is due **3 working days after demobilization**, counted on the working
  /// calendar." Demobilization is §8's and does not exist, so this is set at release from the
  /// ticket's required-by date — see `liquidationDueFrom` in cash-advance-rules.ts, which §8 will
  /// call again with the real date.
  liquidationDueAt DateTime?
  liquidatedAt     DateTime?

  amountLiquidated Decimal? @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  amountReturned   Decimal? @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  amountReimbursed Decimal? @db.Decimal(14, 2)

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket       Ticket?                  @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project      Project?                 @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])
  liquidations CashAdvanceLiquidation[]

  @@index([status, liquidationDueAt])
  @@index([ticketId])
  @@index([requestedById, status])
}

/// The reconciliation of an advance (§5).
///
/// Its own model rather than columns on `CashAdvance` because §5 gives it a review cycle of its own
/// — submitted, under review, approved or rejected — and a rejected liquidation is resubmitted
/// rather than overwritten. Two attempts at reconciling one advance are two records, and the
/// register needs to be able to show both.
model CashAdvanceLiquidation {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  cashAdvanceId String
  submittedById String
  submittedAt   DateTime @default(now())

  /// [{ date, category, description, amount, receiptFileId, hasOfficialReceipt }] (§5).
  ///
  /// `hasOfficialReceipt` is separate from having *a* receipt on purpose: a BIR official receipt is
  /// what makes a cost deductible, and a bus ticket is not one. Module 05 needs to tell them apart
  /// when these post as project costs.
  lines Json @default("[]")

  totalSpent          Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  balanceReturned     Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 2)
  balanceReimbursable Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 2)

  /// draft | submitted | under_review | approved | rejected (§5).
  status String @default("submitted")

  reviewedById String?
  reviewedAt   DateTime?
  remarks      String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  cashAdvance CashAdvance @relation(fields: [cashAdvanceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@index([cashAdvanceId, submittedAt])
  @@index([status])
}

/// The delivery lane (specs/04-operations-projects.md §13).
///
/// §13: "The flowchart's right-hand column. **Delivery tickets never enter the project lane.**" So
/// this is not a cut-down project — it is its own flow, with its own mobilisation, its own loops and
/// its own completion.
///
/// One per delivery ticket, which is why `ticketId` is unique: the retries are rows inside
/// `attempts`, not repeated flows. Three failed visits and a successful fourth is **one** delivery
/// with four attempts, and §20 asks for exactly that — one DR, three logged failures, and a single
/// `sales_order.goods_delivered`.
model DeliveryTicketFlow {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  ticketId String @unique

  /// The document, which module 03 owns. Plain id across the module boundary, as everywhere else.
  deliveryReceiptId String?

  /// own_vehicle | courier. §13: "chosen when the ticket is generated and can be changed until
  /// dispatch" — after that the choice has consequences in the world.
  mode String @default("own_vehicle")

  /// §13.1 step 1-2: no DR, no mobilisation. The gate is the reason these timestamps exist.
  drRequestedAt   DateTime?
  drRequestedById String?
  drIssuedAt      DateTime?
  drIssuedById    String?

  // ---- own vehicle ----
  mobilizedAt   DateTime?
  demobilizedAt DateTime?
  vehicleRef    String?
  driverName    String?

  /// [{ attemptNo, at, contactPersonSought, contactReached, itemDelivered, drSigned, failureReason,
  ///    photoFileIds, geo }] (§13).
  ///
  /// §13.3 wants failed attempts counted by cause and by site, because "repeated failures at one site
  /// are a fixable process problem, usually a wrong contact, and nobody currently counts them". That
  /// only works if the cause is a code rather than a sentence — see delivery-rules.ts.
  attempts Json @default("[]")

  // ---- courier ----
  courierName          String?
  waybillNumber        String?
  trackingUrl          String?
  bookedAt             DateTime?
  pickedUpAt           DateTime?
  courierPodFileId     String?
  courierDeliveredAt   DateTime?
  courierRecipientName String?

  /// §13.2 step 6: "Courier charges on bulk deliveries are material and routinely forgotten in
  /// margin." Integer centavos, like every other money field.
  freightCost  Int?
  insuredValue Int?

  /// dr_requested | dr_issued | mobilized | attempting | in_transit | delivered_unsigned |
  /// completed | failed | rescheduled.
  ///
  /// `delivered_unsigned` is the one that earns its place. §13 calls it "its own state, and it is a
  /// billing risk" — the goods are with the customer and AIES cannot bill. It is reached from both
  /// modes and escalates on the same clock, because a courier's proof of delivery is not a signed
  /// AIES delivery receipt.
  status String @default("dr_requested")

  /// When the goods reached the customer, whichever mode delivered them. Distinct from `completedAt`:
  /// the gap between the two *is* the billing risk, and it is what the escalation measures.
  deliveredAt         DateTime?
  unsignedEscalatedAt DateTime?

  finalOutcome String?
  completedAt  DateTime?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket Ticket @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])

  @@index([status, deliveredAt])
  @@index([mode, status])
}

/// §6.1's site inspection — the visit itself, as distinct from the ask.
///
/// §6 opens by scoping it: "Only `new_project` tickets take this branch, per the flowchart." But
/// the section then widens it in a sentence that matters more than the sketch above it: "This is
/// **also the sub-flow module 01 calls** when sales requests a pre-quotation inspection — same
/// record type, raised from an inquiry instead of a ticket."
///
/// So §6.1's field list (`ticketId, projectId?, siteId`) is incomplete by its own prose, and this
/// model carries `inquiryId` and `inspectionRequestId` as well. All four origins are nullable
/// because an inspection legitimately arrives by two routes:
///
///  1. **From a ticket**, when a new project needs surveying before work is planned.
///  2. **From an inquiry**, when sales cannot price the job without somebody looking at the site —
///     specs/01-crm-inquiry.md §5, whose `InspectionRequest` is the *request* and this is the
///     *visit*. crm.prisma already anticipated this: "When module 04 lands it consumes
///     `inspection.requested` and this becomes the request of record with the field task
///     alongside it."
///
/// Keeping them one model rather than two is the spec's instruction and also the useful choice: the
/// findings, the photographs and the scope-change flag are identical whichever way the visit was
/// asked for, and a company that inspects a site pre-quotation and again pre-mobilisation wants both
/// visits in one place.
model SiteInspection {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESSIR-{YY}{####} — site inspection report.
  number String @unique

  /// Origins. See the model comment: at least one is always set, enforced by the service rather
  /// than by a check constraint, so the error is a sentence instead of a Postgres violation.
  ticketId            String?
  projectId           String?
  inquiryId           String?
  inspectionRequestId String? @unique
  siteId              String?

  inspectedAt    DateTime?
  /// Who was *assigned* to go, set when the survey is scheduled. Plain ids — the list is read whole,
  /// never queried across, the same choice `Ticket.assignedUserIds` made.
  inspectedByIds String[]  @default([])

  /// Who actually attended: `[{ party, name }]` where party is sales | technical | other.
  ///
  /// Separate from `inspectedByIds` because they are different facts, and conflating them was the
  /// original flaw — the form asked "who attended" and wrote it to the assignment field. Asked for by
  /// the company on 2026-08-17: internal attendance is recorded by department, since that is what
  /// matters for a survey, and anybody else — the client's engineer, a principal's representative —
  /// is named. An `other` with no name is refused; see site-inspection-rules.ts.
  attendees Json @default("[]")

  /// §6.1's free-form record of what was found and what is already there.
  findings           String?
  existingConditions Json    @default("{}")

  /// [{ label, value, unit }] — §6.1's measurements. Json because no two surveys measure the same
  /// things, and a column per dimension would be a migration per site type.
  measurements Json     @default("[]")
  /// Equipment tag numbers seen on site. specs/01-crm-inquiry.md §5 names a tag list as one of the
  /// "required outputs" precisely because coming back for it costs a second visit.
  tagNumbers   String[] @default([])

  accessConstraints  String?
  permitsRequired    String[] @default([])
  hazards            String[] @default([])
  /// { power, air, water, crane, scaffolding } — §6.1's list, as booleans with a note.
  utilitiesAvailable Json     @default("{}")

  /// FileObject ids from module 00 storage.
  photoFileIds  String[] @default([])
  sketchFileIds String[] @default([])

  /// scheduled | completed | approved (§6.1).
  status String @default("scheduled")

  /// §6.1: "**If `scopeChangeIdentified` is true, emit `scope_change.identified`.** … Discovering at
  /// inspection that the job is bigger than quoted is normal; discovering it *after* mobilization is
  /// expensive. This link is one of the highest-value things the platform does."
  ///
  /// A boolean and not an inference. Nothing can reliably derive "this is bigger than we quoted"
  /// from findings text, and a guess here either cries wolf or stays silent on the one that mattered.
  scopeChangeIdentified Boolean @default(false)
  scopeChangeNotes      String?

  /// Set when the event has actually been emitted, so re-saving an inspection that already reported
  /// a scope change does not tell sales twice. Nullable rather than a boolean: the timestamp is the
  /// answer to "when did sales find out", which is the question asked afterwards.
  scopeChangeReportedAt DateTime?

  requestedById String?
  scheduledFor  DateTime?
  completedAt   DateTime?
  approvedById  String?
  approvedAt    DateTime?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket  Ticket?  @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project Project? @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])

  @@index([status, scheduledFor])
  @@index([ticketId])
  @@index([inquiryId])
}

/// §6.2's method statement — how the work will actually be done.
///
/// §6.2: "It is a **controlled document** (module 07), not a free-text field." Module 07 does not
/// exist, so `documentId` is nullable and waits for it. Everything else is here, because the part
/// that matters commercially is not the filing — it is the sentence below.
///
/// §6.2: "**The client approves the methodology before work starts. Always.**" And the reason the
/// dates are modelled as carefully as the content: "Client methodology approval is a common and
/// invisible source of schedule slip, and AIES is usually blamed for delays it did not cause. **A
/// dated submission record changes that conversation.**"
model Methodology {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESMTH-{YY}{####}, seeded since module 00.
  number String

  projectId String?
  ticketId  String?

  title    String
  /// R0, R1, … A rejection by the client creates the next one; see `parentMethodologyId`.
  revision Int    @default(0)

  /// The root of the revision chain, null on R0 — the same shape as Quotation's, and for the same
  /// reason: §6.2 calls the chain "the evidence of what was agreed", and a linked list makes
  /// "show me every revision" a recursive walk.
  parentMethodologyId String?

  scopeSummary   String
  /// [{ step, description, durationHours, crew }] — §6.2's "ordered steps with duration and crew".
  sequenceOfWork Json   @default("[]")
  /// [{ role, count, notes }].
  manpowerPlan   Json   @default("[]")

  /// §6.2: these two "**pre-populate the material request** in §7. Nobody should type the same list
  /// twice." §7 does not exist yet; the shapes are chosen so it can read them without a migration.
  toolsRequired     String[] @default([])
  /// [{ description, quantity, unit }].
  materialsRequired Json     @default("[]")

  safetyPlan String?
  /// The job safety analysis, as an uploaded FileObject.
  jsaFileId  String?

  permitsRequired             String[] @default([])
  environmentalConsiderations String?

  durationDays       Int?
  mobilizationPlan   String?
  demobilizationPlan String?
  contingencyPlan    String?

  /// draft | internal_review | approved | submitted_to_client | client_approved | client_rejected |
  /// superseded (§6.2).
  status String @default("draft")

  preparedById String
  reviewedById String?
  approvedById String?
  approvedAt   DateTime?

  /// §6.2's turnaround, and the whole point of dating it: the gap between these two is the
  /// customer's, and without a record of it the delay is remembered as AIES's.
  submittedToClientAt  DateTime?
  clientApprovedAt     DateTime?
  /// The client's own signed approval. §6.2 gates mobilization on this **as well as** the status —
  /// a status somebody set and a document the client signed are not the same claim.
  clientApprovalFileId String?
  clientRejectionNotes String?

  /// §6.2: "defaults to `true` and the flag exists only so a rare exception can be recorded, not as
  /// a routine setting." Turning it off is an act with a reason attached, not a checkbox.
  clientApprovalRequired Boolean @default(true)
  /// Why the exception was made, when it was. Null whenever `clientApprovalRequired` is true.
  clientApprovalWaiver   String?

  /// Module 07's controlled document. Null until module 07 exists.
  documentId String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  project Project? @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])
  ticket  Ticket?  @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])

  parent   Methodology?  @relation("MethodologyRevisions", fields: [parentMethodologyId], references: [id])
  children Methodology[] @relation("MethodologyRevisions")

  /// One row per revision of a number, exactly like Quotation's.
  @@unique([number, revision])
  @@index([projectId, status])
  @@index([ticketId])
  @@index([status, submittedToClientAt])
}

/// §7's Gate 2 — the materials that have to be in the van before anybody leaves.
///
/// §7 opens by insisting the diamond has three answers, not two: "The flowchart's Y / N/A / N
/// diamond. **All three outcomes are real**: materials needed, not applicable to this ticket, or
/// needed but not yet available." And it closes by saying why the middle one is modelled at all:
/// "`N/A` is a legitimate, recorded answer — **not a skipped step**. The record shows someone
/// decided." Same reasoning as §5's `cashAdvanceRequired` boolean.
model MaterialRequest {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESMR-{YY}{####}, seeded since module 00.
  number String @unique

  ticketId  String
  projectId String?

  requestedById String
  requestedAt   DateTime  @default(now())
  neededBy      DateTime?

  /// draft | pending_approval | approved | partially_issued | issued | purchased | rejected |
  /// cancelled (§7).
  status String @default("draft")

  approvedById    String?
  approvedAt      DateTime?
  rejectionReason String?

  issuedById String?
  issuedAt   DateTime?

  /// §7: "Tool return is tracked on demobilization." Demobilisation is §8's, so this is set when the
  /// request is issued (from the ticket's required-by date) and §8 will correct it to the real one.
  returnDueAt DateTime?
  returnedAt  DateTime?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket  Ticket                @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project Project?              @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])
  lines   MaterialRequestLine[]

  @@index([ticketId])
  @@index([status, neededBy])
}

model MaterialRequestLine {
  id        String @id @default(cuid())
  requestId String
  lineNo    Int

  /// consumable | spare_part | tool | instrument | ppe | rental (§7).
  itemType String

  /// The stock item this draws against, when it is one AIES holds.
  stockItemId String?
  productId   String?
  description String
  quantity    Decimal @db.Decimal(14, 3)
  unit        String  @default("pc")

  /// stock | purchase | customer_supplied | rental (§7).
  ///
  /// `purchase` is the one with a consequence: §7 says those lines "emit
  /// `material.purchase_required` → module 03 raises a purchase request. The ticket sits at
  /// `material_pending` until resolved."
  source String @default("stock")

  qtyIssued   Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 3)
  qtyReturned Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 3)
  qtyConsumed Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 3)

  /// §7: "**Instruments drawn against a ticket are recorded.** This is what makes module 08's
  /// out-of-tolerance impact assessment possible ('which jobs used this instrument since its last
  /// valid calibration?')."
  ///
  /// Module 08 does not exist. The column is here so the link is captured from the first issue
  /// onwards — an impact assessment can only look back over records that were kept at the time, so
  /// starting to keep them late is the one thing that cannot be fixed later.
  calibrationAssetId String?

  /// pending | issued | returned | consumed | purchased | cancelled.
  status String  @default("pending")
  notes  String?

  request   MaterialRequest @relation(fields: [requestId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  stockItem StockItem?      @relation(fields: [stockItemId], references: [id])

  @@unique([requestId, lineNo])
  @@index([stockItemId])
  @@index([calibrationAssetId])
}

/// §7's minimum viable inventory.
///
/// §7 is explicit about the boundary, and it is a boundary worth holding: "It is deliberately **not**
/// the full valuation-and-costing inventory system from Spec.md open question 3 — that remains open.
/// **Track quantity and custody, not weighted-average cost.**"
///
/// So there is no unit cost on this model, no valuation, and no costing method. A quantity and a
/// location answer the question the flowchart actually asks — is it in the store, and who has it —
/// and adding a cost column would invite somebody to build a valuation on a stock file nobody
/// reconciles.
model StockItem {
  id       String @id @default(cuid())
  sku      String @unique
  name     String
  category String @default("consumable")
  unit     String @default("pc")

  qtyOnHand    Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 3)
  reorderLevel Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(14, 3)
  location     String?

  lastCountedAt DateTime?

  /// When this instrument's calibration next falls due.
  ///
  /// §7: "**Drawing an overdue-calibration instrument is blocked.**" Module 08 owns calibration
  /// properly — certificates, history, the out-of-tolerance assessment — and does not exist. This is
  /// the store's own copy of the due date, which is what a storeman needs at the moment of issue,
  /// and it is what the block reads until module 08 keeps it in step. Null for anything that is not
  /// a measuring instrument.
  calibrationDueAt DateTime?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  lines     MaterialRequestLine[]
  movements StockMovement[]

  @@index([category, name])
}

/// Every movement in or out, so `qtyOnHand` is explainable rather than merely current.
///
/// A running quantity with no history is a number nobody can argue with and nobody can trust. The
/// movement rows are what let somebody ask "where did the other four go" and get an answer.
model StockMovement {
  id          String @id @default(cuid())
  stockItemId String

  /// issue | return | adjustment | receipt | consume.
  type     String
  /// Signed: negative takes stock out, positive puts it back.
  quantity Decimal @db.Decimal(14, 3)

  ticketId  String?
  requestId String?
  reference String?

  byId String
  at   DateTime @default(now())

  stockItem StockItem @relation(fields: [stockItemId], references: [id])

  @@index([stockItemId, at])
  @@index([ticketId])
}

/// §8's mobilisation and demobilisation — one model, two directions.
///
/// §8's field list gives them a `type` rather than two models, and that is right: the same crew,
/// vehicle, checklists and odometer readings are recorded going out and coming back, and the useful
/// questions ("who went, in what, when did they leave and return") are asked across both.
///
/// The demobilisation row is the one that closes loops opened four sessions ago. §8: "Demobilization
/// closes the loop: tools returned and reconciled against the material request, site cleared,
/// customer notified, **cash advance liquidation triggered**, crew released." Its `actualAt` is the
/// real demobilisation date that §5's liquidation deadline and §7's tool-return date have been
/// deriving from proxies until now.
model Mobilization {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  ticketId  String
  projectId String?

  /// mobilization | demobilization (§8).
  type String

  plannedAt DateTime?
  actualAt  DateTime?

  /// Plain ids, read whole — the same choice `Ticket.assignedUserIds` made.
  crewIds    String[] @default([])
  vehicleRef String?
  driverName String?

  /// [{ label, checked, note }] — §8's tools and PPE checklists. Json because what is on them
  /// differs by job, and a column per item would be a migration per site type.
  toolsChecklist Json @default("[]")
  ppeChecklist   Json @default("[]")

  /// not_required | pending | obtained (§8). Site access is refused for want of a gate pass more
  /// often than for anything technical, which is why it is a field rather than a note.
  gatePassStatus     String  @default("not_required")
  permitStatus       String  @default("not_required")
  inductionCompleted Boolean @default(false)

  departureOdometer Int?
  arrivalOdometer   Int?

  /// planned | ready | departed | on_site | returned | cancelled.
  status String  @default("planned")
  notes  String?

  photoFileIds String[] @default([])

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket  Ticket   @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project Project? @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])

  @@index([ticketId, type])
  @@index([status, plannedAt])
}

/// §8's daily progress log — what actually happened on site, one row per day.
///
/// §8 asks for "daily progress logging against the methodology's sequence of work — steps completed,
/// percent complete, manpower on site, weather and standby time, issues raised, photos."
///
/// There is no model for it in §8's sketch, which lists only `Mobilization`. This is the shape those
/// words require, and the one field that is not obvious from them is `stepsCompleted` — it holds the
/// **step numbers from the method statement**, not free text, because §8 says "against the
/// methodology's sequence of work" and a percentage nobody can trace back to a step is a number
/// somebody made up.
model DailyProgress {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  ticketId  String
  projectId String?

  /// One row per day per ticket — see the unique index. A second entry for the same day is an edit
  /// of the first, not another log: two contradictory accounts of one day help nobody.
  logDate DateTime @db.Date

  /// Step numbers from `Methodology.sequenceOfWork` that were finished on this day.
  stepsCompleted  Int[] @default([])
  /// 0–100. Cumulative, not daily: the question anybody asks is how far along the job is.
  percentComplete Int   @default(0)

  manpowerOnSite Int     @default(0)
  /// Hours the crew was actually working. Standby is tracked separately below and deliberately not
  /// netted off — a day of eight worked and four standing is not the same as a twelve-hour day.
  hoursWorked    Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(6, 2)

  weather String?

  /// §8's standby, and the reason it is modelled at all: "This is the evidence base for a variation
  /// claim, and **today it exists only in people's memory**."
  standbyHours Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(6, 2)
  /// One of §8's six: client_not_ready | permit_delay | weather | material_shortage |
  /// equipment_failure | access_denied. Null when there was no standby.
  standbyCause String?
  standbyNotes String?

  issuesRaised String?
  notes        String?

  photoFileIds String[] @default([])

  loggedById String

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket  Ticket   @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project Project? @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])

  /// One log per day per ticket. The edit path updates; there is no way to file two.
  @@unique([ticketId, logDate])
  @@index([ticketId, logDate])
  @@index([standbyCause])
}

/// §9's Gate 3 — the QA the **client** performs, not AIES.
///
/// §9 is unambiguous, and it shapes every field below: "**Confirmed: QA is performed and approved by
/// the client, not by AIES.** The customer inspects the work. The Operations Manager records whether
/// they approved it and uploads the customer's own documentation as evidence."
///
/// So there is no internal inspector, no AIES pass mark, and no signature field for one. What is
/// modelled is a *recording* of somebody else's decision, and the evidence is what makes the
/// recording worth anything — §9: "the uploaded client approval is simultaneously the quality
/// record, the release authorisation under clause 8.6, and the best support the company will ever
/// have for the final bill."
model QAApproval {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESQA-{YY}{####}.
  number String @unique

  ticketId  String
  projectId String?

  inspectedAt             DateTime?
  clientInspectorName     String?
  clientInspectorPosition String?

  /// §9: "the toggle the Operations Manager sets". Their record of the client's answer, never their
  /// own opinion of the work.
  approved Boolean

  /// §9: "Where a client does not inspect at all, the Operations Manager records that fact
  /// explicitly rather than leaving the gate blank… **A silently skipped gate and a deliberately
  /// waived one look identical in a database unless you make them different.**"
  ///
  /// This is what makes them different, and makes it queryable — a note buried in `remarks` is not
  /// something anybody can count. Same principle as §7's recorded N/A.
  clientInspected Boolean @default(true)

  recordedById String
  recordedAt   DateTime @default(now())

  /// The client's own documentation. §9 makes at least one **required** when `approved` is true, and
  /// that is a hard block rather than a warning — see qa-rules.ts.
  evidenceFileIds String[] @default([])
  /// client_signed_form | email_confirmation | inspection_report | punch_sheet | other (§9).
  evidenceType    String?

  remarks String?

  /// [{ description, severity, ownerId, dueAt, status, photoFileIds }] (§9).
  ///
  /// Severity matters beyond description: §9 auto-raises an NCR in module 08 for major and critical
  /// defects, and module 08 will read these when it exists.
  defects Json @default("[]")

  /// §9: "Rework rounds are counted and reported. **First-time-right (module 09) is
  /// `reworkRound = 0`.** This is the quality metric that matters most and is currently
  /// unmeasurable." The counter is the whole reason this is a numbered record rather than a flag.
  reworkRound    Int     @default(0)
  reworkTicketId String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket  Ticket   @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project Project? @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])

  @@index([ticketId, reworkRound])
  @@index([approved, recordedAt])
}

/// Testing and commissioning (specs/04-operations-projects.md §10).
///
/// The stage a ticket lands in when the client's QA passes. §10 calls the certificate it produces
/// "a primary billing trigger document", which sets the standard of proof for everything here.
model TestingCommissioning {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESTC-{YY}{####}.
  number String @unique

  ticketId  String
  projectId String?

  startedAt   DateTime  @default(now())
  completedAt DateTime?

  /// [{ tagNumber, loopId, result, remarks }] (§10).
  loopChecks Json @default("[]")

  /// [{ test, criterion, criterionSource, quotationLineId, promiseText, criterionSetAt,
  ///    criterionSetById, measured, unit, measuredAt, measuredById, verdict, remarks }].
  ///
  /// §10: results are compared "against the **specification from the accepted quotation**, not
  /// against a value typed in by the technician". Module 02 stores what was promised as prose, so
  /// there is no number to read automatically — see docs/DECISIONS.md #69. What is enforced instead
  /// is provenance: every criterion says where it came from and when it was fixed, and a criterion
  /// fixed in the same act as its own measurement is marked as such.
  functionalTests Json @default("[]")

  /// Against the quoted performance, same shape and same provenance rules as functionalTests.
  performanceVerification Json @default("[]")

  /// §10: instruments used, for module 08 traceability. §7 already refuses to issue an instrument
  /// whose calibration is overdue; this is the record of which one actually took the reading.
  calibrationAssetsUsed String[] @default([])

  witnessedByCustomer     Boolean @default(true)
  customerWitnessName     String?
  customerWitnessPosition String?

  /// [{ description, severity, ownerId, dueAt, status, raisedAt }] (§10).
  ///
  /// §10: "Critical punch items block project close-out." §12 reads these rather than keeping its
  /// own copy.
  punchItems Json @default("[]")

  /// accepted | accepted_with_punch | rejected. Null until somebody decides — an undecided
  /// commissioning must not read as a passed one (§7's recorded N/A, DECISIONS #65).
  result String?

  /// [{ topic, attendees, durationHours, materialsFileId }] (§10).
  trainingDelivered Json @default("[]")

  certificateFileId String?

  recordedById String

  /// The sign-off is the billing trigger, so it carries the customer's signature rather than AIES's
  /// assertion that they were happy — the same standard §5's receipts, §6.2's approval document and
  /// §9's evidence are held to.
  signedOffById           String?
  customerSignatureFileId String?
  signedAt                DateTime?
  /// Why there is no customer signature, when there is none. Required to sign off without one.
  signOffRemarks          String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket  Ticket   @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project Project? @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])

  @@index([ticketId, startedAt])
  @@index([result, completedAt])
}

/// The installed base (specs/04-operations-projects.md §16), built now because §11 needs it.
///
/// §11's warranty gate "checks the equipment's warranty window", and a gate with nothing to check is
/// the theatre docs/DECISIONS.md #69 refused. Same call as §7's minimum-viable `StockItem`: the
/// fields §11 reads are real from today, the rest of §16 — PM scheduling, service intervals, the
/// renewal loop — is inert until §16 builds it.
///
/// §16: "Every commissioned item becomes an `Equipment` record with certificates and full history."
model Equipment {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  accountId String
  siteId    String?

  tagNumber    String?
  serialNumber String?

  productId    String?
  manufacturer String?
  modelNumber  String?
  description  String

  installedAt         DateTime?
  installedByTicketId String?
  salesOrderId        String?
  commissionedAt      DateTime?
  /// The commissioning record this was created from, so the certificate is one hop away.
  commissionedByTcId  String?

  /// §11 reads these. Null means nobody has recorded a window — which is neither "covered" nor
  /// "expired", and warranty-rules.ts refuses to guess. See docs/DECISIONS.md #71.
  warrantyStart DateTime?
  warrantyEnd   DateTime?
  warrantyTerms String?

  /// Inert until §16: PM scheduling and the renewal loop.
  calibrationDueAt DateTime?
  lastServiceAt    DateTime?
  nextPMDueAt      DateTime?

  location           String?
  processDescription String?
  status             String  @default("active")

  documentIds String[] @default([])

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  account        CustomerAccount @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])
  warrantyClaims WarrantyClaim[]

  @@index([accountId, status])
  @@index([warrantyEnd])
  @@index([serialNumber])
  /// §16's renewal loop sweeps these nightly. Without the indexes each sweep is a full scan of the
  /// installed base, which is the one table that grows forever.
  @@index([status, calibrationDueAt])
  @@index([status, nextPMDueAt])
}

/// §11's warranty gate.
///
/// §11 describes behaviour rather than a model, but it also asks for warranty work to be "reported
/// separately: count, cost, and root cause by product and by technician" — and a report needs
/// something to count. This is that record: the claim, the determination, and what the determination
/// produced.
model WarrantyClaim {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESWC-{YY}{####}.
  number String @unique

  accountId         String
  equipmentId       String?
  /// The work this comes back on. §11: "work already commissioned comes back for rectification."
  originalProjectId String?
  originalTicketId  String?

  reportedAt       DateTime @default(now())
  reportedById     String
  faultDescription String

  /// in_warranty | out_of_warranty | unknown. `unknown` is a real answer, not a missing one — see
  /// warranty-rules.ts and docs/DECISIONS.md #71.
  coverage               String
  coverageDeterminedAt   DateTime?
  coverageDeterminedById String?
  /// Why, when a person overrode what the dates said. An override nobody explains is a blank field.
  coverageOverrideReason String?

  /// aies_caused | customer_caused | third_party | undetermined.
  ///
  /// Deliberately a **separate axis** from coverage, following §8's standby attribution. §11 makes a
  /// defect AIES caused non-billable *and* an NCR — and that is true whether or not the warranty has
  /// expired. Folding the two into one enum would lose the case that matters most: our fault, out of
  /// warranty, still ours to fix.
  attribution String @default("undetermined")

  rootCause         String?
  rootCauseCategory String?

  /// Derived from coverage and attribution, then stored — it is the commercial decision somebody
  /// signed, not a formula anybody may quietly re-run later.
  billable Boolean

  /// The after_sales ticket this raised, when it raised one.
  resultingTicketId String?
  /// §11: an AIES-caused defect "auto-raises an NCR (module 08)". Module 08 does not exist, so the
  /// obligation is recorded here and carried by the event.
  ncrRequired       Boolean   @default(false)
  /// §11: out of warranty "prompts sales to quote the rectification".
  salesReferredAt   DateTime?

  /// open | routed | closed
  status  String  @default("open")
  remarks String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  account   CustomerAccount @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])
  equipment Equipment?      @relation(fields: [equipmentId], references: [id])
  project   Project?        @relation(fields: [originalProjectId], references: [id])

  @@index([accountId, status])
  @@index([coverage, attribution])
  @@index([reportedAt])
}

/// The service report (specs/04-operations-projects.md §12).
///
/// §12: "Customer signs on the technician's device; the PDF emails to the customer on sync and files
/// against the equipment and project."
model ServiceReport {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  /// AIESSR-{YY}{####}.
  number String @unique

  ticketId  String
  projectId String?

  workPerformed   String
  findings        String?
  recommendations String?
  /// [{ description, partNumber, quantity, unit, fromStock, stockItemId }] (§12).
  partsUsed       Json    @default("[]")

  /// The installed base this touched, so the report files against the equipment as §12 asks.
  equipmentIds String[] @default([])

  startedAt      DateTime?
  finishedAt     DateTime?
  travelTimeMin  Int?
  standbyTimeMin Int?

  /// draft | pending_signature | signed | submitted | approved (§12).
  status String @default("draft")

  /// The customer's signature. A report AIES signed alone is AIES's account of its own work — the
  /// same standard §5's receipts, §6.2's approval document, §9's evidence and §10's certificate are
  /// held to.
  customerSignatureFileId String?
  customerName            String?
  customerPosition        String?
  customerRemarks         String?
  /// Why there is no customer signature, where there is none. Required to sign off without one.
  signatureWaiverReason   String?

  technicianSignatureFileId String?
  photoFileIds              String[] @default([])

  followUpRequired Boolean @default(false)
  followUpNotes    String?
  ncrRaised        Boolean @default(false)

  preparedById String
  approvedById String?
  approvedAt   DateTime?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  ticket  Ticket   @relation(fields: [ticketId], references: [id])
  project Project? @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])

  @@index([ticketId, status])
  @@index([projectId, status])
}

/// Project close-out (specs/04-operations-projects.md §12).
///
/// One per project — close-out is a state the project reaches, not an event that can happen twice.
///
/// §12: "Approval emits `project.closed` → module 05 releases final billing. **This is the explicit
/// handover the brief describes.**" Which is why the blockers are computed rather than ticked: a
/// checklist somebody can tick past is not a handover, it is a formality.
model ProjectCloseOut {
  id        String @id @default(cuid())
  projectId String @unique

  /// The blockers as they stood when last computed, kept so the screen can show who owns each one
  /// without recomputing six queries on every render. Recomputed on demand — never trusted as the
  /// answer to "can this close".
  checklist Json @default("[]")

  /// in_progress | submitted | approved
  status String @default("in_progress")

  documentIds String[] @default([])

  submittedById String?
  submittedAt   DateTime?
  approvedById  String?
  approvedAt    DateTime?

  /// §12's blocker: "missing customer acceptance where required". Required by default — the safe
  /// direction, since the effect of the default is to hold rather than to release. Waiving it is a
  /// deliberate act with a reason, not a blank field.
  customerAcceptanceRequired Boolean   @default(true)
  customerAcceptanceFileId   String?
  acceptanceDate             DateTime?
  acceptanceWaiverReason     String?

  lessonsLearned String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  project Project @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id])

  @@index([status])
}

/// specs/04-operations-projects.md §14's outbox, server side.
///
/// **The correctness requirement of the whole section.** §14: "every field write queues locally with
/// a client-generated UUID. Server operations are idempotent on that UUID." A technician in a plant
/// with no signal queues an afternoon of work; the phone reconnects on the drive home and replays
/// it, possibly twice if the first reply was lost on a dying connection. Without this table the
/// second replay creates a second delivery attempt, a second progress log, a second everything.
///
/// It also carries §14's conflict policy, which is the part that is easy to get wrong: "the server
/// rejects only on business-rule violation and surfaces the conflict on next sync — **never silently
/// discards work**". So a rejection is *recorded here* rather than only returned as an HTTP error.
/// An error that exists solely in a response body is gone the moment the tab closes, and the
/// technician learns that four hours of work vanished — which §14 calls out as the thing that
/// destroys trust in the system permanently.
model FieldSubmission {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// Generated on the device, before the write is attempted, and never reused. The unique
  /// constraint is the entire mechanism: a replay collides and returns the first result.
  clientUuid String @unique

  userId String

  /// Which service this replays into — `delivery.attempt`, `progress.log`, and so on. Kept as a
  /// string rather than an enum so a new field write does not need a migration to become syncable.
  operation String

  /// A digest of the payload as submitted. Two submissions sharing a UUID but differing in content
  /// mean a client bug or a reused id, and answering the second with the first's result would hide
  /// it. Recorded so it can be detected rather than assumed away.
  payloadHash String

  /// applied | rejected
  ///
  /// There is deliberately no `pending`: a row is written inside the same transaction as the work,
  /// so it exists only once the outcome is known. A crash mid-write leaves no row and the replay
  /// runs normally, which is the safe direction.
  status String

  /// What the write produced, so a replay can answer identically without doing the work twice.
  result Json?

  /// Why a business rule refused it, in the words the technician should see. Never null when
  /// `status` is `rejected` — a rejection with no reason is the silent discard §14 forbids.
  rejectionReason String?

  /// What it touched, for the audit trail and for the client to navigate to.
  entityType String?
  entityId   String?

  /// Set once the client confirms it has shown the outcome to the person. An applied write nobody
  /// saw and a rejected one nobody saw are different problems, and only the second is urgent.
  acknowledgedAt DateTime?

  /// When the device recorded it, as distinct from when the server received it. The gap is how long
  /// the work sat in a bag with no signal, and §14's storage guard needs it to reason about age.
  capturedAt DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  @@index([userId, status])
  @@index([userId, acknowledgedAt])
  @@index([operation, createdAt])
}

/// specs/04-operations-projects.md §15's checklist template.
///
/// §15 opens by saying what this replaces: "the undocumented, verbal way work is currently
/// confirmed". A technician says the loop checked out and everybody believes them, and six months
/// later — when a customer disputes it, or an ISO auditor asks — there is nothing to read.
///
/// **A published version is immutable.** §15: "templates are versioned; responses permanently record
/// the version used, so historical evidence reflects the procedure actually in force." That sentence
/// only holds if a published version cannot be edited underneath the responses that cite it.
/// Changing an active template means publishing a new version; the old one is retired, never
/// rewritten. Otherwise a signed-off checklist could silently come to mean something its signer
/// never agreed to, which is worse than having no checklist at all.
model ChecklistTemplate {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// Stable across versions — `site_inspection` stays `site_inspection` at v1 and v7, which is what
  /// lets a screen ask for "the current site inspection checklist" without knowing the number.
  key     String
  version Int    @default(1)

  name        String
  description String?

  /// Which stage of the flowchart this belongs to, from §15's seeded list. Used to offer the right
  /// checklist on the right screen rather than making somebody pick from eleven.
  stage String

  /// [{ key, title, items: [{ key, label, type, required, ... }] }].
  ///
  /// Json rather than tables because a template is read and rendered whole, never queried by item —
  /// and because the item shapes differ per type (a numeric has tolerances, a select has options).
  /// Normalising that would mean a column per type, mostly null. See checklist-rules.ts for the
  /// shapes and the parser that refuses a malformed one.
  sections Json @default("[]")

  /// draft | active | retired. Only `active` may be answered; `draft` is editable; `retired` is kept
  /// because responses point at it.
  status String @default("draft")

  publishedAt   DateTime?
  publishedById String?
  retiredAt     DateTime?

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  responses ChecklistResponse[]

  /// One row per version of a key. The constraint is what makes "publish a new version" the only way
  /// to change a published procedure.
  @@unique([key, version])
  @@index([key, status])
  @@index([stage, status])
}

/// One filled-in checklist. §15's evidence, and the thing §12's service report assembles from.
model ChecklistResponse {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  templateId      String
  /// Denormalised deliberately: a response has to say which procedure it followed even if the
  /// template row is later soft-deleted, and reading it should not require a join to find out.
  templateKey     String
  templateVersion Int

  /// The sections and items exactly as they were when this was answered.
  ///
  /// Belt and braces over the immutability rule above, and worth the duplication: this is ISO 9001
  /// evidence, and evidence that can only be read by joining to another table is evidence that
  /// depends on that table still being right. A snapshot can be read on its own in five years.
  snapshot Json @default("{}")

  /// What it was filled in against. Plain ids — a checklist attaches to a ticket, a project, or a
  /// delivery flow, and hard relations to all three would be three nullable foreign keys and a
  /// check constraint nobody maintains.
  ticketId   String?
  projectId  String?
  entityType String?
  entityId   String?

  /// { [itemKey]: { value, na?, note?, photoFileIds?, cause?, action? } } — see checklist-rules.ts.
  ///
  /// An absent key and a recorded `na` are different facts, and §15 makes that a *type* distinction
  /// by offering both `pass_fail` and `pass_fail_na`: on the first, "not applicable" is not an answer
  /// anybody may give. docs/DECISIONS.md — the same principle as §7's diamond and §10's witness.
  answers Json @default("{}")

  /// draft | complete
  status String @default("draft")

  startedById   String?
  startedAt     DateTime  @default(now())
  completedById String?
  completedAt   DateTime?

  /// §15 lists `signature` as an item type; this is the checklist-level sign-off, which is a
  /// different claim: not "this reading is 4.2mA" but "I certify this whole procedure was followed".
  signatureFileId  String?
  signedByName     String?
  signedByPosition String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  template ChecklistTemplate @relation(fields: [templateId], references: [id])

  @@index([ticketId, status])
  @@index([projectId, status])
  @@index([templateKey, completedAt])
  @@index([entityType, entityId])
}

/// specs/04-operations-projects.md §16's hours.
///
/// One row per person per day per ticket. The four buckets are separate columns rather than a total
/// with a type, because §8's standby is already a fact the platform records and argues about — a
/// crew waiting at a gate for a permit is a cost the customer may owe, and a total that has absorbed
/// it cannot be broken back out when somebody asks.
model Timesheet {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  /// One or the other, usually both. Plain ids, like everything else that crosses a lane.
  ticketId  String?
  projectId String?

  userId String
  date   DateTime @db.Date

  regularHours  Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(6, 2)
  overtimeHours Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(6, 2)
  travelHours   Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(6, 2)
  /// §8's standby, which is why these are four columns and not one.
  standbyHours  Decimal @default(0) @db.Decimal(6, 2)

  activity String?
  notes    String?

  /// draft | submitted | approved | rejected
  status         String    @default("draft")
  approvedById   String?
  approvedAt     DateTime?
  rejectedReason String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  /// One sheet per person per day per ticket. A second row for the same day is an edit, not a new
  /// fact, and without this a duplicate submission doubles somebody's week.
  @@unique([userId, date, ticketId])
  @@index([userId, date])
  @@index([ticketId, status])
  @@index([projectId, status])
  @@index([status, date])
}

/// §16's field spend.
///
/// **The link to a cash advance is the point.** §16: "field expenses linked to a cash advance flow
/// into its liquidation automatically." §5's liquidation currently asks somebody to retype what they
/// spent from a pile of receipts; an expense recorded where it happened, against the advance it came
/// from, is the same fact captured once.
model FieldExpense {
  id String @id @default(cuid())

  ticketId      String?
  projectId     String?
  /// When set, this expense counts toward that advance's liquidation.
  cashAdvanceId String?

  userId String
  date   DateTime @db.Date

  /// See expense-rules.ts. Kept as a string so a new category does not need a migration.
  category String

  /// Integer centavos, like every other money field in the platform.
  amount   Int
  currency String @default("PHP")

  description String

  /// §5 requires a receipt for anything above its threshold; the rules layer decides which.
  receiptFileIds String[] @default([])

  /// draft | submitted | approved | rejected | reimbursed
  status         String    @default("draft")
  approvedById   String?
  approvedAt     DateTime?
  rejectedReason String?
  reimbursedAt   DateTime?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  @@index([cashAdvanceId, status])
  @@index([ticketId, status])
  @@index([projectId, status])
  @@index([userId, date])
}

/// §16's preventive maintenance contract — "where the recurring revenue in this business lives".
///
/// Two jobs, and they are different. It **generates work**: PM visits become `after_sales` tickets N
/// days ahead of schedule, so a contract the company sold does not quietly go unserved. And it
/// **generates leads**: a contract inside its last 90 days is a renewal conversation module 01 should
/// be having, not a surprise when it lapses.
model MaintenanceContract {
  id     String @id @default(cuid())
  number String @unique

  accountId String
  siteId    String?

  startDate DateTime @db.Date
  endDate   DateTime @db.Date

  /// How many visits the contract owes per year. The scheduler divides the term by this.
  visitsPerYear Int @default(1)

  /// Reserved for §17's dispatch rules — "every second Tuesday", quarter-ends, plant shutdowns.
  /// Empty means evenly spaced, which is what `plannedVisitDates` computes today.
  scheduleRule Json @default("{}")

  /// The installed items this covers. Plain ids: equipment moves between contracts on renewal, and a
  /// join table would make that a migration rather than an edit.
  equipmentIds String[] @default([])

  /// Integer centavos.
  contractValue Int    @default(0)
  currency      String @default("PHP")

  salesOrderId String?

  /// draft | active | expired | cancelled | renewed
  status String @default("draft")

  /// Set when the renewal loop has already raised this one, so it is raised once rather than nightly.
  renewalFlaggedAt DateTime?
  /// The contract this was renewed into, so the history reads forward.
  renewedIntoId    String?

  version Int @default(0)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?
  deletedBy String?

  account CustomerAccount @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])

  @@index([accountId, status])
  @@index([status, endDate])
}
